WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760
Welcome back.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.400
Let's see where the mirror equation comes from, instead of treating it as a

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:05.740
password.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.320
Our picture is a concave spherical mirror, a real upright object beyond the

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.900
focus, and a real inverted image.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.320
For this derivation only, capital U, V and F are positive distance magnitudes

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.400
measured from the pole.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.780
Object height is h, and inverted image height magnitude is H.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.460
One ray goes from the object tip to the pole.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.925
Equal angles of reflection give two similar triangles, so H divided by h

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:31.380
equals

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.340
V divided by U.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.680
A second ray travels parallel to the axis and reflects through the focus.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.960
In the paraxial approximation its reflection point is effectively in the pole

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.220
plane, at height h.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.160
On its reflected path, the height falls from h to zero over distance F, then

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.040
from zero to minus H over distance V minus F.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.840
Those similar triangles give H divided by h equal to V minus F, divided by F.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.900
Set our two ratios equal.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.620
V over U equals V over F minus one.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.480
Divide by V and rearrange: one over U plus one over V equals one over F.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.340
This is why both ray geometry and the equation locate the same image.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.440
Now translate, don't mix, sign conventions.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.880
With incident light rightward and the pole at zero, this real concave mirror

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.420
case has u equals minus U, v equals minus V, and f equals minus F.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.200
The Cartesian result is one over u plus one over v equals one over f.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.900
Signed magnification is minus v over u.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.180
Try u minus thirty centimeters and f minus ten.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.740
The equation gives v minus fifteen, and magnification minus one half.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.640
A two centimeter upright object gives an inverted one centimeter image.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.840
Now why is focal length half the spherical radius?

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.240
A near axis parallel ray meets a normal tilted by a small angle theta.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.680
Reflection turns the return ray through twice that angle relative to the axis.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.520
Using tangent theta approximately theta, the same ray height divided by focus

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.080
distance is twice height divided by radius.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.240
Thus F is R magnitude over two.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.220
Wider rays violate that small angle step and can produce spherical aberration.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.767
We have explained the formula and its limits, not claimed that every ray from

00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:11.820
a

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.820
wide spherical mirror shares one exact focus.

00:02:15.048 --> 00:02:17.988
Let's build the curved refraction equation from Snell's law.

00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:22.468
Light travels left to right, from index n one to index n two.

00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:25.008
Draw a surface convex toward a real object on the left.

00:02:25.568 --> 00:02:29.528
Its center is on the right, and for our pictured case a real image is also on

00:02:29.528 --> 00:02:29.828
the right.

00:02:30.248 --> 00:02:33.288
A ray meets the surface a small height h above the axis.

00:02:33.828 --> 00:02:38.508
The incoming ray rises by angle alpha; the outgoing ray falls by angle beta.

00:02:39.088 --> 00:02:42.868
The radius through the point of incidence supplies the normal, whose rightward

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:44.588
direction falls by angle gamma.

00:02:45.228 --> 00:02:49.708
Therefore incidence angle is alpha plus gamma, while refraction angle is gamma

00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:51.488
minus beta in this configuration.

00:02:52.468 --> 00:02:56.208
Snell's law says n one sine incidence equals n two sine refraction.

00:02:56.828 --> 00:03:00.028
At small angles in radians, replace each sine by its angle.

00:03:00.608 --> 00:03:05.108
We get n one times alpha plus gamma equal to n two times gamma minus beta.

00:03:05.668 --> 00:03:10.808
Move terms: n one alpha plus n two beta equals n two minus n one, times gamma.

00:03:11.368 --> 00:03:13.248
Now connect angles to distances.

00:03:13.808 --> 00:03:19.186
Alpha is approximately h over minus u, beta is h over v, and gamma is h over

00:03:19.186 --> 00:03:19.328
R.

00:03:20.148 --> 00:03:20.748
Divide by h.

00:03:21.128 --> 00:03:26.628
The result is n two over v minus n one over u equals n two minus n one over R.

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:30.557
Unlike a mirror, light proceeds into the second medium, and both indices

00:03:30.557 --> 00:03:30.868
belong

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:31.448
in the equation.

00:03:32.148 --> 00:03:37.308
Let's check the existing example: n one one, n two one point five, u minus

00:03:37.308 --> 00:03:39.948
sixty centimeters, R plus twenty.

00:03:39.948 --> 00:03:42.408
The curvature term is one fortieth per centimeter.

00:03:43.328 --> 00:03:48.008
Subtract one sixtieth to find one point five over v equal to one one hundred

00:03:48.008 --> 00:03:48.528
twentieth.

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.508
Thus v is plus one hundred eighty centimeters.

00:03:50.508 --> 00:03:54.708
Pause: a longer image distance is reasonable here because this surface only

00:03:54.708 --> 00:03:56.328
weakly converges the incident beam.

00:03:57.108 --> 00:03:58.208
Let R tend to infinity.

00:03:58.708 --> 00:03:59.848
The curvature term vanishes.

00:04:00.588 --> 00:04:04.421
For an object twenty centimeters inside water of index four thirds, viewed

00:04:04.421 --> 00:04:04.628
near

00:04:04.628 --> 00:04:09.363
normally from air through a plane surface, set u minus twenty and swap n one

00:04:09.363 --> 00:04:09.488
to

00:04:09.488 --> 00:04:11.208
four thirds, n two to one.

00:04:11.728 --> 00:04:15.739
The equation gives v minus fifteen centimeters: a virtual image closer to the

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:16.208
boundary.

00:04:16.928 --> 00:04:20.328
Our sketch's positive small angles are a route to the equation for that

00:04:20.328 --> 00:04:21.188
pictured configuration.

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:25.842
Virtual images or reversed curvature require signed geometry, not an

00:04:25.842 --> 00:04:26.388
assumption

00:04:26.388 --> 00:04:28.528
that every angle keeps the pictured sign.

00:04:29.188 --> 00:04:33.348
Keep the pole, incident direction and media labels visible in a JEE solution.

00:04:33.504 --> 00:04:36.704
A telescope formula depends on where the final image is.

00:04:37.224 --> 00:04:41.191
Normal adjustment means final image at infinity: relaxed viewing,

00:04:41.191 --> 00:04:41.984
magnification

00:04:41.984 --> 00:04:46.144
minus objective focal length over eyepiece focal length, and refractor lens

00:04:46.144 --> 00:04:48.124
separation their sum.

00:04:48.124 --> 00:04:52.304
Now put the final virtual image at the near point, a distance D from the

00:04:52.304 --> 00:04:53.564
eyepiece in our ideal model.

00:04:53.764 --> 00:04:56.644
We neglect separation between eye and eyepiece.

00:04:57.024 --> 00:05:00.844
D is twenty five centimeters for the standard reference eye, not a fixed

00:05:00.844 --> 00:05:01.984
property of every person.

00:05:02.624 --> 00:05:05.004
The eyepiece image distance is minus D.

00:05:05.684 --> 00:05:10.744
Its thin lens equation gives one over f e equal to minus one over D minus one

00:05:10.744 --> 00:05:12.184
over u e.

00:05:12.184 --> 00:05:15.664
Therefore the intermediate image lies on its incoming side at distance

00:05:15.664 --> 00:05:20.524
magnitude s equal to f e times D divided by D plus f e.

00:05:20.524 --> 00:05:21.984
This is less than f e.

00:05:21.984 --> 00:05:26.524
The objective still forms its intermediate image approximately f o from itself

00:05:26.524 --> 00:05:27.804
for a distant object.

00:05:28.244 --> 00:05:32.664
So lens separation is f o plus s, slightly less than the normal adjustment

00:05:32.664 --> 00:05:33.064
length.

00:05:33.904 --> 00:05:37.715
The incoming object angle makes intermediate height magnitude approximately f

00:05:37.715 --> 00:05:37.764
o

00:05:37.764 --> 00:05:38.584
times that angle.

00:05:39.444 --> 00:05:43.044
The outgoing angle at the eyepiece is approximately that height divided by s.

00:05:43.924 --> 00:05:49.655
Hence signed magnification is minus f o over s, or minus f o over f e times

00:05:49.655 --> 00:05:49.884
one

00:05:49.884 --> 00:05:50.964
plus f e over D.

00:05:51.324 --> 00:05:55.884
Let's use f o one hundred centimeters, f e five, and D twenty five.

00:05:55.884 --> 00:06:00.444
s is twenty five sixths centimeters, about four point one seven.

00:06:00.444 --> 00:06:03.164
Lens separation is one hundred four point one seven centimeters.

00:06:04.184 --> 00:06:07.824
Magnification is minus twenty four, compared with minus twenty and separation

00:06:07.824 --> 00:06:10.144
one hundred five centimeters in normal adjustment.

00:06:11.284 --> 00:06:14.434
Greater angular magnification here needs accommodation; it is not

00:06:14.434 --> 00:06:15.064
automatically

00:06:15.064 --> 00:06:16.904
the most comfortable or clearest view.

00:06:18.024 --> 00:06:21.901
Think before swapping in the microscope formula: the microscope's nearby

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:22.224
object

00:06:22.224 --> 00:06:25.464
and objective linear magnification create a different first stage.

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:29.844
For a reflecting telescope, the concave primary replaces the refracting

00:06:29.844 --> 00:06:33.144
objective and the eyepiece uses the same final image reasoning.

00:06:33.904 --> 00:06:37.624
A two hundred centimeter primary and four centimeter eyepiece give normal

00:06:37.624 --> 00:06:39.344
magnifying power magnitude fifty.

00:06:40.224 --> 00:06:44.144
Do not equate that optical focal length sum to every reflector's physical tube

00:06:44.144 --> 00:06:46.564
length: a secondary can fold the beam.

00:06:47.384 --> 00:06:50.384
Magnification, aperture, resolution and brightness remain distinct.

00:06:51.284 --> 00:06:55.364
These are ideal paraxial classroom calculations, not instructions to view the

00:06:55.364 --> 00:06:57.724
Sun or build unprotected optical equipment.
